  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nLatest MCS is departing to our east with a few isolated showers and \nlight stratiform rain briefly in its wake. Will let the Flood Watch \nrun as planned due to lingering activity, but overall the threat of \nany additional flooding is largely trending downward.\nUnfortunately, this predominant northwest flow continues to promote \na low confidence forecast over the next 48 hrs, particularly in \nregards to timing and coverage of shower and storms. The overnight \nwill be primarily quiet, but additional showers and storms are \npossible as we head into the late morning and per latest CAMs. We \nwill remain in an environment with high PWs but due to little to no \nsurface instability, thus, storms would likely be elevated and \nstruggle to produce much in terms of a wind threat. Localized \nflooding cannot be ruled out. Allowing the Flood Watch to continue \nas planned will also allow us to see how radar trends play out into \nthe morning, as CAMs are currently doing a very poor job with some \nspotty CI occurring over northern KY. \n\nAn additional round of showers and storms is possible this evening, \nsimilar to tonight. Again, models are highly variable in coverage(or \ngeneral presence) and timing of this activity. The warm and moist \nsummer airmass could allow for MUCAPE near 2k J/kg and mid-level \nlapse rates 5.5-6.0 degC per latest HRRR soundings. The shortwave \ninfluence would also allow for effective shear near 20-25kts. A few \nstrong or maybe isolated severe storms cannot be ruled out, \nprimarily close to our northeast Tennessee into southwest Virginia \ncounties. With repeated rounds of showers and storms over the past \nweek, localized flooding could also occur with the strongest \nactivity. Additional rounds showers and storms cannot be ruled out \ninto Friday, but confidence further lessens as what happens with the \npreceding storms can impact evolution. \n\nHowever, we will start to see a warming trend Friday into the \nweekend as upper level heights see a gradual increase as a ridge \naxis shifts eastward. For the time being it looks like some isolated \nlocations could approach Heat Advisory criteria Friday afternoon. As \nwe head into the weekend, there will be an increasing chance for \nportions of the southern and central valley to see heat indices in \nthe 100-107 degree range. A Heat Advisory may need to be considered \nas we get closer in time. Saturday through Monday look to be the \nmost hot and humid days. While some precip chances linger into the \nweekend, they are primarily confined to the higher elevations like a \nmore typical summer time day where a few isolated showers and storms \ncould drift into the valley as the afternoon goes on. increasing \ncoverage of PoP chances and slightly cooler temperatures are the \ntheme Tuesday into mid-week as an amplifying trough in the Great \nLakes region attempts to weaken the southeastern ridge.",
